#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# raster

#% text
# `raster(cells)` is a graphics primitive: a grid of coloured cells, one per element of the matrix cells, the first row at the bottom, filling the rectangle from (0, 0) to (columns, rows).

#% text
# `raster(cells, [[x0, y0], [x1, y1]])` stretches the grid over the rectangle with these corners.

#% text
# `raster(cells, rect, g)` colours numbers with the gradient g instead of gray.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A cell is a number from 0 to 1 (a gray from black to white, or a colour of the gradient), a list `[r, g, b]` or `[r, g, b, a]`, a colour such as `red`, or `none` for a transparent cell.

#% item
# Each cell is one flat colour, with no smoothing between cells, in PNG, SVG and PDF alike.

#% item
# A raster with numbers that are not yet known stays as it is; other wrong cells give a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A small checkerboard:

#% cell
graphics(raster([[0, 1, 0], [1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 0]]))
#% output
#= image("raster.files/raster-1.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Colours, and a transparent cell showing the disk behind:

#% cell
graphics([orange, disk([1, 1], 0.9), raster([[red, none], [[0, 0, 1], [0, 0.6, 0.3, 0.5]]], [[0, 0], [2, 2]])])
#% output
#= image("raster.files/raster-2.svgz")

#% text
# Numbers coloured by a gradient:

#% cell
graphics(raster(table(mod(i*k, 7)/6, i, 7, k, 7), [[0, 0], [1, 1]], viridis), frame: true)
#% output
#= image("raster.files/raster-3.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics`, `list_density_plot`, `gradient_color`
